# Out of Season - a seaside guest house, a guest who does not leave, and a story told in the THIRD person.
# Written for the library: one of forty-one stories before it was firmly second-person (two-of-everything is the only
# other third-person one), so the narrative stance is what changes here rather than the surface. The choices are hers.

title: Out of Season
start: start

== start ==
bg hall_corner
music room_tone
The man in room four arrives on the second of October with one bag and a laptop, and Marion gives him
the key and the wifi code and does not think about him again for nine days.
The guest house holds eleven and it is down to three by the middle of the month, which is what the
season does: the families go at the end of August, the couples go when the schools go back, and what
is left is walkers, one or two weddings, and whoever has decided to be somewhere else for a while.
-> the_week

== the_week ==
He pays for a week on the Friday, in cash, and then another week on the next Friday, and by the third
Friday she has stopped asking whether he wants the same room.
He is in the breakfast room at half past eight every morning and out by nine, in all weather, walking,
and back at four, and in the evening he sits in the front room with the laptop and does not put the
television on. He is polite in the specific way people are polite when they do not want to be asked
anything, and she has been doing this for nineteen years and knows the difference.
-> the_ledger

== the_ledger ==
There is a thing she has never done in nineteen years, which is to say no to a booking in October.
She has taken the season's money and the season's people and she has never once sat down with the
ledger in the October and worked out what the last three weeks of the year actually cost, because
the last three weeks of the year are hers: the boiler gets serviced, the gutters get done, and the
front room is hers to sit in.
This year she has worked it out. Three weeks of one guest is three hundred and fifteen pounds and it
is worth less than the boiler service.
* Say nothing and take the money. The season is the season and he is polite. -> take_it
* Ask him, at breakfast, how long he is planning to stay. -> ask_him
* Tell him the house closes on the fifth of November, which is true. -> close_it

== take_it ==
She says nothing and he pays on the fourth Friday and again on the fifth, and the house is quieter
than she has ever known it in the October, and the front room is not hers.
She starts doing the gutters in the mornings instead, because there is nothing else to do and because
the front room is occupied, and it rains on her twice in the first week and she gets the job done
anyway, and on the eighth morning she stops and stands on the path with the ladder up and thinks the
thing she has been not thinking, which is that she is doing the gutters in the rain in the October to
avoid her own front room.
-> the_decision

== ask_him ==
She asks him on the Saturday, at breakfast, with the tray of toast in her hand, and he does something
she has not seen him do before, which is to look at the table.
man: Until you close, if that's all right.
marion: We don't close.
man: I know. I looked.
-> the_answered

== close_it ==
She tells him on the Monday that the house closes on the fifth of November, which is true and which
is also four days earlier than the week she usually stops and two days later than he has paid for.
He says thank you and pays for the last week that afternoon, in cash, and is gone by the Thursday,
and the bed in room four is stripped and turned and left, and the front room is hers from the
fourteenth of October onward, which has never happened.
-> the_quiet_season

== the_answered ==
man: I'm not a guest who's going to become a problem. I'm just - I'm between two things, and the
walking is the point, and I'd rather be here than in a flat.
He says the word here the way people say the name of a place they have decided about.
~ knows 1
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
Which is the moment the season's question arrives, and it arrives with the tray still in her hands:
whether she is a woman who lets a man pay three hundred and fifteen pounds for a room in her house in
the October, or whether she is a woman who has a house in the October.
* Let him stay. Three hundred and fifteen pounds is three hundred and fifteen pounds. -> he_stays
* Tell him he can stay to the fifth but the season price ends, and say why. -> reprice
* Tell him the truth about the front room, and that she does the gutters in the rain. -> the_truth [if knows >= 1]

== he_stays ==
He stays to the end of the month and then to the middle of the November, and she closes the house on
the twentieth with him still in it, which means letting a room in a closed house, which she has never
done.
He leaves on the twenty-second with the one bag and a thank you, and the room takes her four hours to
set right, and on the following Tuesday she sits in the front room for the first time that year with a
cup of tea and the rain on the windows and finds that she is not sure what to do in it any more.
-> END

== reprice ==
She tells him on the Saturday afternoon, standing in the hall with the ledger in her hand, that the
season price ends on the fifth and that a room in a closed house in the November is seventy pounds a
week, which is not a number she has ever said out loud.
He says that's fair, and pays it, and stays to the twenty-second, and it turns out that the number was
the whole of it: it was never about the money past a certain point, it was about whether she had said
it, and saying it cost her nothing and gave her the house back for the last three weeks, when nobody
was in the front room in the evening but her.
-> END

== the_truth ==
She puts the tray down and sits down opposite him, which she has not done in three weeks, and tells
him about the gutters in the rain and about the front room and about what the last three weeks of the
year have meant for nineteen years, and he listens to the whole of it without saying anything.
Then he tells her why he is here, which takes four minutes and which she does not repeat to anybody,
ever, including her sister, and which changes nothing about the money and everything about the
morning.
-> END

== the_quiet_season ==
The season is quiet and hers. The gutters get done in the second week of the November in dry weather
with the radio on in the yard, and the boiler is serviced on the eighteenth by a man she has used for
eleven years, and the front room has the fire on and nobody in it but her, and she reads four books
in three weeks, which she has not done since she was thirty.
In the February a card comes to the house addressed to her by name, with no message in it, and it has
a photograph of a coast path on the front, about forty miles from here, which is to say a different
one. She puts it on the shelf in the front room where she can see it from the chair.
-> END
